package Lessons

use Libraries.Web.Page.all
use Libraries.Web.Page.Standards.ComputerScienceStandards2026
use Libraries.Web.Page.Standards.QuorumStandards

class ScenePalette is QuorumPage
    // The site's sets of standards, built once for every page.
    QuorumStandards standards

    on create
        SetLocation("tutorials/Scenes/palette.html")
    end

    action Setup
        // For search engines and link previews.
        SetDescription("Learn about changing the Palette in Quorum Studio")

        Title("Changing the Palette")

        Overview()
        WarmUp()
        Explore()
        Engage()
        WrapUp()
    end

    action Overview
        Section("Overview")
        Paragraph("The palette is the list of things that can be placed in a scene. Quorum Studio lets anyone
add their own 2D images to it, give each one a name and a description, and set some default
properties. Each set of images is a pair of files: a picture holding many small images, and a text
file that describes them. This page explains that text file, field by field, and how to credit the
artists whose images are used.")

        Subsection("Goals")
        Goals()
        ListItem("Explain how a tileset's image and its JSON file work together.")
        ListItem("Describe each field of a tileset file, and which ones are required.")
        ListItem("Add a tileset to the palette, with a source and license that credit its creator.")
    end

    action WarmUp
        Section("Warm up")
        Paragraph("Imagine a sheet of stickers with no labels, and a friend who can't see the sheet asks for "):Quoted("the third one in the second row"):Text(". What would you need to write down so anyone could find, name and describe every sticker on the
sheet?")

        Subsection("Vocabulary")
        Paragraph("You will be learning about the following vocabulary words:")
        Table("Vocabulary", "media/scenes/Palette/Vocabulary.csv")

        Subsection("CSTA Standards")
        ComputerScienceStandards2026 computerScience = standards:GetComputerScience2026()
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:HIGH_SCHOOL_DATA_COLLECTION_AND_PREPARATION_22)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:HIGH_SCHOOL_PROGRAM_DEVELOPMENT_14)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:SPECIALTY_1_GAME_DEVELOPMENT_PROFESSIONAL_PRACTICE_8)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:SPECIALTY_1_GAME_DEVELOPMENT_PLAYER_EXPERIENCE_5)
        StandardsList(computerScience)
    end

    action Explore
        Section("Explore")
        ChangingThePalette()
        WhereTilesetsLive()
        FindingImagesAndGivingCredit()
        ATilesetFile()
        TheFieldsForTheWholeTileset()
        TheFieldsForEachTile()
        MoreTilesFromTheSameImage()
        WhatAScreenReaderUserHears()
    end

    private action ChangingThePalette
        Subsection("Changing the Palette")
        Paragraph("Quorum Studio allows adding images to the palette, setting some default properties on them, and
giving them names and descriptions. These are special image sheets, loaded by Quorum's scene editor
when Quorum Studio starts. There are defaults, but a project may need different graphics. To add
them, files tell Quorum Studio what to show and how to label it.")
        Paragraph("This page is about 2D images. 3D models are added in a different way, from a folder named
Tilesets3D, and aren't covered here.")
    end

    private action WhereTilesetsLive
        Subsection("Where Tilesets Live")
        Paragraph("Apart from the built-in items, such as the cube and the terrain, every item in the palette comes
from a pair of files. In the folder where Quorum Studio is installed there is a folder named
Resources, and in it a folder named Tilesets. Each pair there has:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem():Strong("a PNG file,"):Text(" which holds a set of images on a grid;")
        ListItem():Strong("a JSON file,"):Text(" a text file that tells Quorum Studio which part of the image is each item, and how to name and
describe it for screen readers.")
        Paragraph("Quorum Studio reads the Tilesets folder once, when it starts. After adding or changing a tileset,
close and reopen Quorum Studio to see it in the palette. If a file has a mistake, such as a missing
X or a number written as 1.0 instead of 1, the whole tileset is skipped with no message. If a
tileset doesn't appear, check its file first.")
        Paragraph():Strong("The game needs the image too."):Text(" A scene saves the path of each tile's image, such as "):Code("Resources/Tilesets/Characters.png"):Text(", and the game loads it from the project's folder when it runs. A new scene project has its own
Resources/Tilesets folder with copies of the default tilesets. Copy a new tileset's PNG into the
project's Resources/Tilesets folder as well, or the game won't find it.")
    end

    private action FindingImagesAndGivingCredit
        Subsection("Finding Images, and Giving Credit")
        Paragraph("There are many free tilesets online. The default images in Quorum Studio come from tilesets found on "):Link("itch.io's free tilesets", "https://itch.io/game-assets/free/tag-tileset"):Text(". Tilesets you draw yourself can be added too.")
        Paragraph("Before using someone else's images, read their license. It says what you may do with them and
whether you must credit the creator. The tileset file has a place to record both, so the credit
travels with the images.")
    end

    private action ATilesetFile
        Subsection("A Tileset File")
        Paragraph("Once an image is chosen and saved to the Tilesets folder, its JSON file is written and saved there
too. Here is part of the file for the characters in the default palette. Its image, Characters.png,
is a grid of 24 by 24 pixel squares: a boy running across the top row and a girl across the bottom
row.")
        JsonExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/scenes/ScenePalette/Characters.json")
    end

    private action TheFieldsForTheWholeTileset
        Subsection("The Fields for the Whole Tileset")
        Paragraph("This is a regular JavaScript Object Notation (JSON) file. Names are case-sensitive: "):Code(JsonName("X")):Text(" works, "):Code(JsonName("x")):Text(" doesn't.")
        Table("The Fields for the Whole Tileset", "media/scenes/Palette/TilesetFields.csv")
    end

    private action TheFieldsForEachTile
        Subsection("The Fields for Each Tile")
        Paragraph("Each tile has a label, such as "):Code(JsonName("0")):Text(", followed by its fields in curly braces. The label only has to be different for each tile; Quorum
Studio doesn't use it and it isn't shown. Counting up from 0 keeps the file tidy.")
        Table("The Fields for Each Tile", "media/scenes/Palette/TileFields.csv")
    end

    private action MoreTilesFromTheSameImage
        Subsection("More Tiles from the Same Image")
        Paragraph("When more images are used from the same PNG file, repeat a tile's fields, with a new label, for each
one. Unlike in the scene editor, where items can go anywhere, the positions here are on a set grid.
The size of a square is the Width and Height at the top of the file, and X and Y count squares, not
pixels.")
        Paragraph("So in the file above, the next image to the right of the boy is at "):Code(JsonName("X") + ": 1"):Text(" and "):Code(JsonName("Y") + ": 0"):Text(", and the image below him, the girl, is at "):Code(JsonName("X") + ": 0"):Text(" and "):Code(JsonName("Y") + ": 1"):Text(".")
    end

    private action WhatAScreenReaderUserHears
        Subsection("What a Screen Reader User Hears")
        Paragraph("In the palette, the tree reads the tileset's Name, then the Category, then each tile's Name: "):DoubleQuote():Output("Characters 2D"):DoubleQuote():Text(", "):DoubleQuote():Output("Boy"):DoubleQuote():Text(", "):DoubleQuote():Output("1: Boy"):DoubleQuote():Text(". Placing a tile is announced as "):DoubleQuote():Output("Placed 1: Boy"):DoubleQuote():Text(". This is why clear names and descriptions matter: for someone using a screen reader, they are the
image.")
        Paragraph("Not every property a scene item can have can be set in a tileset file. What is supported may grow
over time, based on what people ask for.")
    end

    action Engage
        Section("Engage")
        Paragraph("For this final section, enjoy working on your terrain. See if you can give your level to a partner
for feedback.")
    end

    action WrapUp
        Section("Wrap up")
        Paragraph("The JSON file is a small data dictionary: it says what each value means and which values are
allowed. Which fields did the computer need, and which were there for people: players, screen reader
users and the artist who made the images?")
        Paragraph("This is the last tutorial in the scene series. Back to the start: "):Link("Introduction to Scene Editing", "/tutorials/Scenes/intro.html"):Text(".")
    end

    // A name as written in a JSON file, in its quote marks, which can't be
    // typed inside a Quorum string.
    private action JsonName(text name) returns text
        text mark = ""
        mark = mark:GetDoubleQuote()
        return mark + name + mark
    end
end
